      "case_id": "agent_case_02",
      "answer": "The workspace contains one paper that compares one-stage and two-stage object detectors:\n\n- **\"You Only Look Once: Unified, Real-Time Object Detection\"** (work_id: `da37b10b-f5e6-4b74-9659-cc66e4abec26`)\n\nThis paper introduces YOLO, a one-stage detector, and compares it to two-stage approaches such as Fast R-CNN and Deformable Parts Models (DPM). Key points from the comparison include:\n\n- YOLO is significantly faster than most two-stage detectors, processing images at 45 frames per second (or 155 fps for Fast YOLO).\n- While YOLO makes more localization errors, it is less likely to predict false positives on background compared to Fast R-CNN.\n- The paper highlights architectural differences: two-stage methods like R-CNN use region proposal followed by classification, whereas YOLO frames detection as a single regression problem.\n\nThe comparison is detailed in sections such as \"3. Comparison to Other Detection Systems\" and \"4.2. VOC 2007 Error Analysis\".\n\nNo other works in the workspace discuss this comparison.",
