      "answer": "The workspace contains the original YOLO paper but no RetinaNet-related papers. Therefore, a direct comparison of common methods between YOLO and RetinaNet cannot be made from the current corpus.\n\nHowever, based on the YOLO paper present in the workspace:\n\n- YOLO frames object detection as a **single regression problem**, directly predicting bounding boxes and class probabilities from full images in one evaluation.\n- It uses a **unified convolutional neural network** that enables end-to-end optimization.\n- The model is **extremely fast**, processing images at 45 fps (or up to 155 fps in the Fast YOLO variant), making it suitable for real-time applications.\n- YOLO learns **generalizable representations** of objects, showing robustness when applied to domains like artwork, outperforming methods such as DPM and R-CNN in such scenarios.\n\nSince RetinaNet is not represented in the workspace, no shared or contrasting methods can be identified from the available materials.",
